{"p":"/ui/size-modifiers","t":"Size modifiers and constraints","d":"Explains how use size modifiers and size constraints with user interface objects.","h":"<p>Alongside basic <a href=\"#/docs/ui/position-and-size\">sizing</a> of UI objects, you can utilize size modifiers to <a href=\"#scale\">scale</a> an object proportionally or <a href=\"#automatic-sizing\">automatically resize</a> it. You can also insert <a href=\"#constraints\">size constraints</a> to control <strong>aspect ratio</strong>, or set a minimum and maximum <strong>size</strong> or <strong>text size</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Scale</h2>\n<p>A <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UIScale\"><code>UIScale</code></a> object stores a numerical value that multiplies the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiBase2d/GuiBase2d.AbsoluteSize\"><code>AbsoluteSize</code></a> property of the parent <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObject</code></a>. For example, if you want an object to be twice as large as it currently is, you can insert a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UIScale\"><code>UIScale</code></a> object with a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UIScale/UIScale.Scale\"><code>Scale</code></a> property of <code>2</code>.</p>\n<p>This modifier is useful for \"zooming in\" while designing a detailed user interface in Studio, since it proportionally scales the object and all of its children, including any applied <a href=\"#/docs/ui/appearance-modifiers\">appearance modifiers</a> like <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UIStroke\"><code>UIStroke</code></a> or <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UICorner\"><code>UICorner</code></a>. It's also useful to <a href=\"#/docs/ui/animation\">tween</a> the size of an object, for example to slightly increase the size of a button when a player hovers their mouse over it.</p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<h2>Automatic sizing</h2>\n<p>The <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.AutomaticSize\"><code>AutomaticSize</code></a> property automatically resizes a parent <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObject</code></a> to the size of its descendants. You can use this property in a variety of cases, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Expanding a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObject</code></a> to fit text that has been <a href=\"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/localization\">localized</a> in many languages.</li>\n<li>Allowing users to <a href=\"#/docs/ui/text-input\">input text</a> within a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextBox\"><code>TextBox</code></a>, automatically adjusting its size based on the amount of text entered.</li>\n<li>Automatically adjusting the size of text objects using <a href=\"#/docs/ui/rich-text\">rich text markup</a>, including font type and size.</li>\n</ul>\n<video class=\"gmedia\" controls preload=\"none\" src=\"g/assets/ui/automatic-sizing/Intro-Frame.mp4\" data-alt=\"\"></video>\n\n<p>You can enable the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.AutomaticSize\"><code>AutomaticSize</code></a> property for any <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObject</code></a>. By default, it is set to <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/enum/AutomaticSize\"><code>None</code></a>, but you can change its value to have specific control over which direction(s) the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObject</code></a> resizes to fit its content.</p>\n<div class=\"callout c-info\">\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ScrollingFrame\"><code>ScrollingFrame</code></a> uses the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ScrollingFrame/ScrollingFrame.AutomaticCanvasSize\"><code>AutomaticCanvasSize</code></a> property instead of <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.AutomaticSize\"><code>AutomaticSize</code></a>. It behaves the same but resizes the frame's <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ScrollingFrame/ScrollingFrame.CanvasSize\"><code>CanvasSize</code></a>, not its base <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.Size\"><code>Size</code></a>.</p>\n</div>\n<p>To enable automatic sizing:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>In the <a href=\"https://create.roblox.com/docs/studio/explorer\">Explorer</a> window, click any <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObject</code></a>, such as a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Frame\"><code>Frame</code></a>, <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextBox\"><code>TextBox</code></a>, or <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ImageLabel\"><code>ImageLabel</code></a>.</li>\n<li>\n<p>In the <a href=\"https://create.roblox.com/docs/studio/properties\">Properties</a> window, navigate to the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.AutomaticSize\"><code>AutomaticSize</code></a> property (<a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ScrollingFrame/ScrollingFrame.AutomaticCanvasSize\"><code>AutomaticCanvasSize</code></a> for <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ScrollingFrame\"><code>ScrollingFrame</code></a>) and set it to one of the following options:</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/enum/AutomaticSize\"><code>X</code></a> &mdash; Resizes content horizontally.</p>\n</li>\n<li><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/enum/AutomaticSize\"><code>Y</code></a> &mdash; Resizes content vertically.</li>\n<li><a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/enum/AutomaticSize\"><code>XY</code></a> &mdash; Resizes content both horizontally and vertically.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" /></p>\n<p>Once automatic sizing is set, note that other object properties behave as follows:</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\">\n<div class=\"tab\" data-label=\"AnchorPoint\">\n<p>Similar to the concept of <a href=\"#/docs/ui/position-and-size\">sizing</a> an object, <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.AutomaticSize\"><code>AutomaticSize</code></a> takes the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.AnchorPoint\"><code>AnchorPoint</code></a> property into consideration when resizing content. For example, the following video displays three <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextLabel\"><code>TextLabels</code></a> that automatically resize from their anchor point.</p>\n<video class=\"gmedia\" controls preload=\"none\" src=\"g/assets/ui/automatic-sizing/customizing-anchorpoint.mp4\" data-alt=\"\"></video>\n<ul>\n<li>The top label has an <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.AnchorPoint\"><code>AnchorPoint</code></a> value of <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/datatype/Vector2/Vector2.new\"><code>(0, 0.5)</code></a> which anchors it to the middle of the left side.</li>\n<li>The middle label has an <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.AnchorPoint\"><code>AnchorPoint</code></a> value of <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/datatype/Vector2/Vector2.new\"><code>(0.5, 0.5)</code></a> which anchors it to the center.</li>\n<li>The bottom label has an <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.AnchorPoint\"><code>AnchorPoint</code></a> value of <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/datatype/Vector2/Vector2.new\"><code>(1, 0.5)</code></a> which anchors it to the middle of the right side.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div class=\"tab\" data-label=\"Size\">\n<p>When you enable <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.AutomaticSize\"><code>AutomaticSize</code></a>, the object's <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.Size\"><code>Size</code></a> property controls its <strong>minimum size</strong>. This means that the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObject</code></a> cannot resize smaller than its core size along any axis.</p>\n<p>For example, the following white <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Frame\"><code>Frame</code></a> has a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.Size\"><code>Size</code></a> of <code>0</code> for both axes, meaning it doesn't have a minimum width/height and can resize according to its child objects.</p>\n<video class=\"gmedia\" controls preload=\"none\" src=\"g/assets/ui/automatic-sizing/customizing-size-0.mp4\" data-alt=\"\"></video>\n<p>In comparison, the following white <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Frame\"><code>Frame</code></a> has a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.Size\"><code>Size.X</code></a> of <code>100</code>, so it cannot resize to a width less than 100 pixels.</p>\n<video class=\"gmedia\" controls preload=\"none\" src=\"g/assets/ui/automatic-sizing/customizing-size-100.mp4\" data-alt=\"\"></video>\n</div>\n<div class=\"tab\" data-label=\"TextWrapped\">\n<p>When you enable <strong>text wrapping</strong> for a UI text object (<a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextLabel/TextLabel.TextWrapped\"><code>TextLabel</code></a>, <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextButton/TextButton.TextWrapped\"><code>TextButton</code></a>, or <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextBox/TextBox.TextWrapped\"><code>TextBox</code></a>) and set the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.AutomaticSize\"><code>AutomaticSize</code></a> property to either <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/enum/AutomaticSize\"><code>Y</code></a> or <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/enum/AutomaticSize\"><code>XY</code></a>, the label resizes to accommodate any text that wraps across multiple lines. For example, the following <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextLabel\"><code>TextLabel</code></a> has <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextLabel/TextLabel.TextWrapped\"><code>TextWrapped</code></a> enabled and an <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject/GuiObject.AutomaticSize\"><code>AutomaticSize</code></a> of <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/enum/AutomaticSize\"><code>Y</code></a>, so when text overflows the width and wraps, the label expands vertically.</p>\n<video class=\"gmedia\" controls preload=\"none\" src=\"g/assets/ui/automatic-sizing/customizing-textwrapping.mp4\" data-alt=\"\"></video>\n</div>\n</div>\n<h2>Constraints</h2>\n<p>There are three types of <strong>constraints</strong> you can use for a user interface object: <a href=\"#size\">size</a>, <a href=\"#text-size\">text size</a>, and <a href=\"#aspect-ratio\">aspect&nbsp;ratio</a>. To use a constraint, you must set it as a <strong>child</strong> of the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObject</code></a> you want to constrain.</p>\n<h3>Size</h3>\n<p>The <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UISizeConstraint\"><code>UISizeConstraint</code></a> specifies a minimum and maximum size for a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObject</code></a>. This constraint ensures that the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObject</code></a> doesn't become too small or large on different screen sizes.</p>\n<p>For example, if you set the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UISizeConstraint/UISizeConstraint.MinSize\"><code>MinSize</code></a> property to <code>(200, 200)</code> and the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UISizeConstraint/UISizeConstraint.MaxSize\"><code>MaxSize</code></a> property to <code>(400, 400)</code>, the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObject</code></a> cannot scale smaller than 200&times;200 pixels or larger than 400&times;400 pixels.</p>\n<div class=\"callout c-warning\">\n<p>When a UI object is under control of both a layout structure such as <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UIListLayout\"><code>UIListLayout</code></a> and a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UISizeConstraint\"><code>UISizeConstraint</code></a>, the constraint will <strong>override</strong> the layout and control the object's size.</p>\n</div>\n<h3>Text size</h3>\n<p>The <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UITextSizeConstraint\"><code>UITextSizeConstraint</code></a> specifies a minimum and maximum font size for a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObject</code></a> with text, such as a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextLabel\"><code>TextLabel</code></a>, <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextButton\"><code>TextButton</code></a>, or <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextBox\"><code>TextBox</code></a>. This constraint ensures that the <strong>text</strong> within a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObject</code></a> doesn't become illegible or too large.</p>\n<p>If you enable the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextLabel/TextLabel.TextScaled\"><code>TextScaled</code></a> property of the parent <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObject</code></a>, the text size scales with the container's size and respects constraints even if the object becomes smaller or larger than the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UITextSizeConstraint/UITextSizeConstraint.MinTextSize\"><code>MinTextSize</code></a> and <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UITextSizeConstraint/UITextSizeConstraint.MaxTextSize\"><code>MaxTextSize</code></a> values.</p>\n<p>For example, the following <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextLabel\"><code>TextLabel</code></a> object has a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UITextSizeConstraint\"><code>UITextSizeConstraint</code></a> with a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UITextSizeConstraint/UITextSizeConstraint.MinTextSize\"><code>MinTextSize</code></a> value of <code>50</code> and a MaxTextSize value of <code>80</code>. Even when the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextLabel\"><code>TextLabel</code></a> becomes smaller, the font never becomes smaller than 50 pixels, and when the object becomes large, the font next exceeds 80 pixels.</p>\n<video class=\"gmedia\" controls preload=\"none\" src=\"g/assets/ui/ui-objects/UITextSizeConstraint.mp4\" data-alt=\"\"></video>\n\n<div class=\"callout c-error\">\n<p>Do not use <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UITextSizeConstraint/UITextSizeConstraint.MinTextSize\"><code>MinTextSize</code></a> property values lower than <code>9</code> or the text will be difficult to read for many viewers.</p>\n</div>\n<h3>Aspect ratio</h3>\n<p>The <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UIAspectRatioConstraint\"><code>UIAspectRatioConstraint</code></a> enforces a <strong>width‑to‑height</strong> aspect ratio on a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/GuiObject\"><code>GuiObject</code></a> regardless of its core size, even if that size is set as a percentage of its parent. For example, inserting this constraint as a child of a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Frame\"><code>Frame</code></a> and setting the constraint's <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UIAspectRatioConstraint/UIAspectRatioConstraint.AspectRatio\"><code>AspectRatio</code></a> property to <code>2</code> (<code>2:1</code>) keeps the frame's width at twice that of its height. Similarly, setting this constraint's <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UIAspectRatioConstraint/UIAspectRatioConstraint.AspectRatio\"><code>AspectRatio</code></a> property to <code>0.5</code> (<code>0.5:1</code>) keeps the frame's width at half that of its height.</p>\n<p>Setting this constraint's <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UIAspectRatioConstraint/UIAspectRatioConstraint.AspectRatio\"><code>AspectRatio</code></a> to the default of <code>1</code> (<code>1:1</code>) is a convenient way to prevent non‑proportional scaling/stretching of an <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/ImageLabel\"><code>ImageLabel</code></a> with a square image asset, such as an <a href=\"https://create.roblox.com/docs/projects/assets#rbxthumb\">avatar thumbnail</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"callout c-warning\">\n<p>When a UI object is under control of both a layout structure such as <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UIListLayout\"><code>UIListLayout</code></a> and a <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/UIAspectRatioConstraint\"><code>UIAspectRatioConstraint</code></a>, the constraint will <strong>override</strong> the layout and control the object's size.</p>\n</div>","kid":{"simple":"This page shows tools that help your UI stay the right size on every screen. `UIScale` makes an object and everything inside it bigger or smaller all at once. `AutomaticSize` lets a box grow to fit its contents, like a speech bubble that stretches for long messages. Constraints set limits, such as \"never smaller than this\" or \"always keep this shape\", so your UI does not get squished or huge on phones and big monitors.","analogy":"Constraints are like the rules for a stretchy T-shirt: it can stretch to fit, but it will never shrink below a small size or grow past an extra-large, and it keeps its T shape.","keyPoints":["`UIScale` multiplies the size of its parent and all its children; `Scale` of 2 makes everything twice as big.","`UIScale` is great for hover effects (tween it from 1 to 1.1) and for zooming in while designing.","`AutomaticSize` (X, Y, or XY) makes an object grow to fit its contents; `Size` then becomes the smallest it can be.","With `TextWrapped` on and `AutomaticSize` set to Y, a text label grows taller as text wraps onto new lines.","Scrolling frames use `AutomaticCanvasSize` instead of `AutomaticSize`.","`UISizeConstraint` sets a `MinSize` and `MaxSize` in pixels.","`UITextSizeConstraint` keeps text between `MinTextSize` and `MaxTextSize`; never go below 9 or text gets hard to read.","`UIAspectRatioConstraint` keeps the width-to-height shape; `AspectRatio` 2 means twice as wide as tall. Constraints override layouts like `UIListLayout`."],"terms":{"AbsoluteSize":"The real size of a UI object on the screen, in pixels, after all scale and offset math is done.","UIScale":"A modifier that multiplies its parent's size. 0.5 is half size; 1 is normal; 2 is double.","AutomaticSize":"Lets an object grow to fit what is inside it, across (X), down (Y), or both (XY).","minimum size":"The smallest something can become. With `AutomaticSize` on, `Size` X of 100 means it never gets thinner than 100 pixels.","constraint":"A rule object placed inside a UI element that limits its size or shape.","aspect ratio":"Width divided by height. A 200 by 100 box has an aspect ratio of 2.","TextScaled":"Makes text grow or shrink to fill its box.","localized":"Translated into other languages. A word can be much longer in German than in English, so boxes may need to grow.","override":"To take control instead of something else. A constraint wins over a layout when deciding size."},"example":{"title":"Grow a button a little when hovered","place":"LocalScript inside a TextButton (inside a ScreenGui in StarterGui)","code":"local TweenService = game:GetService(\"TweenService\")\n\nlocal button = script.Parent\n\n-- UIScale lets us resize the button and its contents together\nlocal uiScale = Instance.new(\"UIScale\")\nuiScale.Scale = 1\nuiScale.Parent = button\n\n-- Keep the same wide shape on every screen\nlocal aspect = Instance.new(\"UIAspectRatioConstraint\")\naspect.AspectRatio = 3 -- 3 times as wide as tall\naspect.Parent = button\n\nlocal tweenInfo = TweenInfo.new(0.15) -- quick, 0.15 seconds\n\nbutton.MouseEnter:Connect(function()\n\tTweenService:Create(uiScale, tweenInfo, { Scale = 1.1 }):Play() -- 10% bigger\nend)\n\nbutton.MouseLeave:Connect(function()\n\tTweenService:Create(uiScale, tweenInfo, { Scale = 1 }):Play() -- back to normal\nend)","explain":["We add a `UIScale` to the button, starting at 1 (normal size).","We add a `UIAspectRatioConstraint` so the button always stays 3 times wider than it is tall.","When the mouse moves onto the button, we tween `Scale` to 1.1, making it 10% bigger.","When the mouse leaves, we tween it back to 1."]},"watchOut":["With `AutomaticSize` on, `Size` is the minimum. If you set it big, the object will never shrink below that.","A size or aspect ratio constraint overrides list and grid layouts, which can surprise you when items stop lining up.","Keep `MinTextSize` at 9 or higher so text stays readable.","Mouse hover events do not happen on touch screens, so do not hide important info behind a hover effect."]}}